ining 73 000 of  claim our entries are likely not in htmd  because clinvar does not necessarily  require users  who are submitting their clinical  adaptations about a variance you have  specific guidelines for evidence to  support that assertion  now you can also see that um  hcmd has a large amount of content that  is not available in either omen or  clinvar  now one thing to note is that hmd is for  german mutations only  not for somatic so hmd is a great tool  when you're asking the question does  this human germline mutation cause  disease  now many of you are probably aware that  there is a public version of h  gmd  and so here let's take a look at the  advantages of having a professional  version  i like the public version which is three  years out of date  htmt professional is updated quarterly  with information coming from more than  2000 different journals  and is carried out manually by genetic  experts  who read the literature and bring in the  information  and so here on the right you can see the  statistics between this year  and the fourth quarter release of 2021  for hcmd  and you can see that there is quite a  bit of content that was just added  between these two quarters  uh currently  htmd includes annotations for more than  362 000 variants  all kinds of mutations represent a  growth of about 9 500 mutations within  one quarter  so you can see that  having the professional version compared  to the public version  um  gives you the most up-to-date clinical  information for these variants  now hdmt entries include the first  published example of the mutation  and the pathogenic effect  and certain mutations may also include  additional reports like functional  studies or additional phenotypes  if these reports enhance the original  entry  and so there are a couple of ways that  you can access this information  hcmd can be accessed online  and with aged with the hdmd professional  version you have advanced search  capabilities that are not necessarily  available with the public version  so as you can see here  you have the ability to search by gene  or mutation  you can do phenotype searches  and you can also  look at an overview of  limitations for a specific gene  to get an idea of the number of  mutations by varying type  in addition to the online version  a downloadable  version of hmd professional is also  available  so you prefer to have the data in tab  dna  files or vcf files that's also available  now we're going to look at some examples  that illustrate how hdmd is a  particularly useful tool during your  variant classification workflow  specifically we'll look at how  convenient hemd is when researching a  variant's clinical significance  ways in which visualizing variants  through hdmd can provide information  about the allele of interest that you're  looking at  and then we'll also look at ways to  identify genes associated with specific  phenotypes  and then how you can utilize some of the  advanced abilities to design an ngs  panel  now when it comes to  interpreting variants  legacy and alternative naming schemes  can make researching a variant's  clinical significance kind of difficult  so let's say that you're given  this variant  in gene subpoena a subpoena one  to look over  now a workflow for this might be the  following  using the human genome variation society  hgbs monoclature  for this variant  the first thing you do is go to clinvar  but because you don't necessarily want  to take the entries in quinblar at face  value you dig a little deeper into the  entry  and see the literature  associated for that variant  uh looking at the clinvar entry for this  um serine look at codon six leucine  change  there's no mention of an alternative  names or other schemes  so using this literature information  that's associated with the clinvar entry  you go to pubmed to investigate it  further  and so looking at the associated paper  um the only serine to leucine change  mentioned is happening at codon minus  19.  in addition to this you notice that  this is somewhat also associated with  something called piz  rexam  so at this point you are not seeing any  mention  of a serine  6 leucine  so the next thing you do is go to the  gene review  for subpoena one where you confirm that  alpha one anti-trypsin deficiency is the  associated phenotype  and you get a little bit of background  information about this gene  you see that variants were initially  described based on electrophoretic  protein variants with the prefix  pi  denoting protease inhibitor so  essentially they would add this pi in  conjunction with the name of the  location where this variant was  identified  and all of this was before they knew  that subpoena one was the associated  gene  so here not only do you have what  appears to be  a legacy name  where the codon is different but you  also have this  alternative meaning  scheme based on the location of where  the variant was found  however at this point  uh you're still not quite sure  that you can confirm that  serine 6 leucine  is in fact the same variant as serine  minus 19 that lucy  and so um you look at the omen entry and  you get some similar information  the same literature is associated  uh the same names are given  nonetheless you still don't have any  confirmation that um this  is the same variant you're looking for  and so at this point you suspect that it  is but to be certain  you would have to do uh additional  digging to confirm that the information  you are finding is relevant to the  variant you're interested in  so overall you can see how  time-consuming this workflow can be  now let's look at how using hcmd as your  first step in your variant  classification workflow provides you  with that background information and  saves you the time spent worship  researching these legacy and alternative  naming schemes  and so with htmd  you can search for gene  subpoena  one  and look at this sense of variance  and so here within the first entry  you see the variant you're interested in  steering six smoothing and conveniently  hcmd has a special column  that describes the legacy change so here  you can see  that there is an offset of 24 amino  acids explaining why you are seeing this  minus a 19.  this is probably part of a translocation  signal or something and the original  group studying this variant started  counting 24 codons downstream  and so you have confirmation  that  serine 6 leucine  is in fact the same variant as serine  minus 19 lucy  you can click on this  hdmd assertion  to view the variant entry  and so in this  variant entry you get confirmation that  the associated disease or phenotype is  alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency  hdmd considers this variant a  disease-causing mutation  and then again you have the codon number  and the legacy codon number is also  available in this view  you also have  the primary literature reference for the  first time this variant was published as  well as an additional reference  and so at this point you can be  confident that when you are talking  about this  serine six leucine  it's the same thing mentioned in this  primary literature  and this is because the genetic experts  who are reviewing the information and  updating the content within htmd will  not associate literature  if there is any ambiguity  they will follow up with the authors of  the publication  if there are any questions  so they have done the background digging  for you  saving you time so you can move forward  with your research  now let's look at another use case let's  say that you are given uh this variant  in gene uh gna  so here you're looking at a arginine  297  glutamine change so let's go ahead and  search this in hgmt  and so when you log in to  hcmd professional online this is what  the home page looks like  here you have the current version  you have a list of the contents that are  available and any important  announcements or updates will also be  listed  now there are a number of ways to search  you can search itunes  so here we click on gene  we'll get the option to search by gene  so here we can enter our gene of  interest  and then use this drop down menu to  select  the parameter that we're interested in  and then submit the query  once you do that we can simply select  our gene  and so with a gene search  you can look at all the mutations  within gene  e  and then you can narrow it down by  imitation type of interest so if you're  interested in this sense mutations  you can click on that  and then  scroll down until you find the one that  you're interested in  now another way is to search by mutation  which directly pulls up the varying  information so here you click on  mutation  uh you can search by codon  if you have the hgmd a session  you can directly look it up  uh you can search also by dbsnp as well  now for today we are going to search by  chromosomal coordinates  and so using this little search bar up  here you can either type in or paste in  your coordinates so i'm going to go  ahead and paste those in  and then use  this drop down menu to specify how we  are searching  so we're going to search by  hg38 coordinates  and then click on submit  and so this directly  pulls up the variant you are interested  in  here you can click on the gene symbol  and get the same spectrum of mutations  that you get when you search by the gene  function  you also have the option to link out to  db snip  and you can also review the htmt  assession for this variant so let's go  ahead and do that  and so looking at this assession uh you  can see that the associated phenotype is  sileria  the hmd entry is citing this as a  disease causing mutation  and then  you don't see anything in the codon  number about a legacy naming scheme  here in the literature  citation section  uh you can see that there are a couple  of different papers listed  uh the for the first report was in a  portuguese girl who had the clinical  biochemical and molecular  characteristics  and in addition to the primary  literature  these papers are also included because  the reviewers found that these papers  added useful information  now something that users like you find  particularly helpful  is this comments and notes section  and so here reviewers will add notes to  help clarify things  so as an example we are looking at a 297  code on change  but within this leroy paper  it indicates that it's a 266 code on  change  and then looking at the note for another  publication of the reviewers lets you  know that this paper also reports it the  same way  and gives the ensemble transcript  now another thing that is helpful is  that many times the comments will tell  you where to look for the variant  so if it's only listed in the  supplemental table or the supplemental  data  it will point you in that direction  and so this shows the value of having  someone manually look at the information  not only are they reviewing the body of  the text but they are also able to look  at graphs figures supplemental tables  and they point you to where they found  that key piece of information for the  variant  now with this information you know that  there is a reporting scheme on different  transcripts than the one that you've  been given so hdmd has this section  called additional predicted  hgvs descriptions  feature and so if you click on the  little arrows  you can see the drop down menu appears  and here  it lists all of the rest transcripts the  hgbs transcriptions as well as the  ensemble transcripts  and so this use case highlights how  reviewing the hdmd entry first gives you  key insights that will that will be  helpful as you continue with your  literature review  so if you were to look for additional  literature  for this a specific variant  uh you would know that there is a  possibility that it is reported a number  of different ways so you would need to  be aware when you are doing that search  and how you form that search  so we've looked at how hmd can help you  with your variant research and save you  time along the way  now we'll look at some use cases for  downloading and visualizing hmd data  so when you have a professional license  for hdmd download there are there are  several different files that are  available to you  um here highlighted is the zipped file  for the vcf or hmd professional  in hg 38 but it's also available in hg19  as well  so before moving to the use case i'll  briefly go over how you would go about  uploading this data for visualization  and so here you would download  the vcf file  and then upload the vcf file to a  browser for this many users like you  will either use the integrative genomics  viewer igb or the uc santa cruz genome  browser  and so to give you an idea this is what  the upload of the hemg professional bcf  looks like in igp  if we zoom in on the information here  this is information parsed from the vcf  file you can see the gene  uh the phenotype  the  hgbs non-equature so you can use this to  get the key information  and this is what the upload of the hdmd  professional bcf looks like in the uc  santa cruz genome browser  this allows you to see the nature of the  variant change so you can see if you  have  a deletion  for example  or if you have  an insertion  you can also see if you have a  substitution  as well so it provides you with just a  little bit more  information  and then  this is what the parse information from  the bcs would look like in the  uc santa cruz genome browser  so let's go ahead and see how we can use  the  hgmd vcs download  so many users like you  when they download the hdmd vcf file  will often use it during their secondary  analysis  they will refer to the vcf from hmd and  annotate their variants  so let's say that you did sequencing and  generated a large list of  variants  now you want to narrow down this list to  look at those that are most likely to be  clinically significant  so one of the things that you can do is  filter by things like allele frequency  or quality matrix  with the hgmd vcs download you can also  annotate with  hdmd and use the annotations to filter  down to a smaller list of variants to  look at in more details  now another use case for the h gmd vcs  download is for visualization purposes  so why is visualization helpful  sometimes you want to see what's in what  we call the genomic neighborhood  this can be visualizing a bcf track for  your variants of interest and by using  the vcf download you're able to see if  there are entries within hmg that are at  the same codon and see if they are  marked as disease-causing mutations or  supposed to use cause mutations  and so  for example  i have uploaded  these  variant tracts  of  my bearing of interest and the  hemg vcs into the uc  santa cruz genome browser  and so here we go to the genome  you can see  both the  the  hdmd vcf file and then  the  file for our variant of interest  and so looking at the browser  we have our variant of interest  right here  and then we can see that there are hdmd  entries at the same location  so if we look at these entries  and we click on the information  you can see that um  hmd is citing this as a disease  contention so having this visual  representation is quite helpful for many  users like you  now other times you may find  visualization helpful if you are looking  at a particular region of the gene  so for example if you are looking at  friendships at the pre-prime end and you  have something that's past the boundary  of nonsense media decay you need  additional evidence from the literature  or from functional studies showing that  that last part of the protein has  significance that is cut off  so being able to see visually an hmd  track showing other reported disease  cost mutations in that location will  point you to some of that evidence  now having the hdmd track loaded can  help confirm your secondary analysis  and make sure that is accurately  calling or describing variants  and so let's say that you uploaded your  variant track  into the uc santa cruz geno browser  your variant of interest here in purple  is within the apc gene  and it's in a region where  there is a little bit of repetition with  this agag motif  you can see that there is a little dash  on top followed by a ga  on the bottom indicating that this is an  insertion of a ga  now by loading the hdmd track on top  which is  what all of these variants on the top  are  you can see that there is an insertion  of a ga  in blue that's nearby or in the genomic  neighborhood  as a person reviewing this variant and  maybe examining the bed or bam files or  the breeds  you might suspect that this insertion  is in fact supposed to be called here  a few bases away downstream because  repetitive regions sometimes there can  be changes in how it is represented in  the bcf versus hgvs  in this case uh you would know to follow  up maybe with finger sequencing for  confirmation to make sure that  this  is in fact the variant you want to look  at and that it doesn't need to be  altered  in your secondary analysis  to reflect something that has been  reported in the literature as a disease  causing mutation  and so overall you can see how having  this visualization makes this type of  analysis faster  now that we have looked at some of the  applications for using the hdmd ecf  download  let's look at how you can utilize hdmd  when you are interested in identifying  genes that associate with a specific  phenotype of interest  for example  let's say that you are on the research  side and you are interested in  discovering what genes are associated  with bipolar disorder  with hcmd you can search  by phenotype  and so here you can do a  boolean  search where it will pull in the actual  entries with the term bipolar  disorder  and search within hcmd phenotypes  so you can quickly find  for the associated bipolar disorder  phenotypes within hcmd  a list of genes to follow up on  now  this is going to provide um a good place  to start as this provides  some guidance into where  to  look  at now let's look at how you can utilize  the content within hmd to define a count  based on a phenotype  now let's say that you want to assign a  panel for cardiomyopathy  so  in this case you would use the advanced  search  and so with that uh you would get a list  of variants  within hdmd that are associated with  cardiomyopathy  which can then be used for your panel  design  so let's go ahead and do this in the  hemd professional  online version  so here we're going to go ahead and  click on the advanced  section  and then within advanced we're going to  select nucleotide substitution plus  and so today  we are interested in searching  by a disease or phenotype  but uh taking a look at these different  parameters you can see that you can  search by different things such as gene  symbol  uh db snap just to give you a couple of  examples  now you can search by um functional  elements so if you want to look at  variants within known transcription  binding sites for example  this can be helpful  you can do  a motif search  if you're looking for splice specific  splice variants or regulatory regions so  you can also search for those as well  now over here  you can see  what is going to be  produced by the search and so here you  can select the different outputs the  information that you want to receive  back  and so right now we have um many things  highlighted but you can manually select  and deselect  whatever it is that you're interested in  this case i'm just going to go ahead and  select them all  to get as much information as possible  and so going back down here since we are  interested in  searching by a disease or phenotype  we're going to go ahead and use this  search bar to enter  our disease of interest  and so here we are going to specify that  we're interested in  cardiomyopathy  and then we're going to go ahead and  submit our query  and so this